  "model": "gpt-5.6-terra",
  "reasoning_effort": "high",
  "prompt": "prompts/physics_committee_terra_round1_v1.md",
  "preferred_configuration": {
    "prize_parts": [
      {
        "ballot_id": "B040",
        "laureates": [
          "Hidetoshi Katori",
          "Jun Ye"
        ],
        "citation": "for the invention and development of optical lattice clocks"
      }
    ]
  },
  "alternatives": [
    "B019",
    "B025"
  ],
  "statement": {
    "case_for": "B040 remains my preferred achievement. The magic-wavelength lattice idea made it possible to exploit an optical transition in many confined neutral atoms without the otherwise destructive trapping shifts. Katori supplied the defining concept, and Ye's experimental realizations and extensions helped make it a reproducible precision instrument. Independent laboratories now obtain extraordinary accuracy and use these clocks for fundamental measurements as well as metrology. This is a particularly satisfying case in which a simple quantum-control insight has repeatedly survived demanding systematic tests.",
    "case_against": "The principal reservation is attribution: optical-frequency metrology and lattice-clock development involved many important groups, so the precise boundary between Katori and Ye's central contributions and those of other clock pioneers requires historical scrutiny. The prize should remain focused on the lattice-clock concept and its decisive realization, not the entire subsequent field of optical clocks.",
    "responses": [
      {
        "member_id": "kroll-stefan",
        "point": "Kroll emphasizes the complementary clarity of Katori's magic-wavelength concept and Ye's decisive realization, together with replication, benchmarking, precision tests, and geodesy.",
        "response": "I agree with that assessment. In particular, the repeated comparison of independent clock systems matters more than impressive nominal precision alone: it shows that systematic effects have become physically understood and controlled. I would nevertheless avoid treating later applications as the basis of the prize; the underlying quantum-control advance is already sufficient."
      },
      {
        "member_id": "johansson-goran",
        "point": "Johansson places circuit QED first, while judging optical lattice clocks a mature and compelling fourth-ranked candidate; he notes that clock advances grew through a broad international technical development.",
        "response": "Circuit QED is a powerful and mature platform, but its relation to recently recognized macroscopic quantum phenomena in circuits calls for especially careful delineation. For B040 the central physical innovation is more sharply bounded: confinement at the magic wavelength converts an optical transition into a many-atom clock without the dominant trapping perturbation. I share Johansson's caution on the wider technical lineage, which is why the citation should be narrowly phrased."
      },
      {
        "member_id": "mehlig-bernhard",
        "point": "Mehlig regards the Aharonov-Bohm effect and Berry phase as profound, experimentally established ideas but questions whether their distinct histories fit naturally into one combined citation.",
        "response": "I find B019 an exceptionally strong alternative and agree that both phenomena changed our physical understanding of quantum phase. Yet the qualification is decisive for present formulation: the Aharonov-Bohm effect and Berry phase are distinct discoveries with different co-discovery and experimental histories. B040 offers a cleaner single achievement and a more readily defensible two-person attribution."
      }
    ],
    "uncertainties": [
      "Whether the proposed two-person attribution adequately distinguishes the original lattice-clock concept from other essential optical-clock and metrological contributions."
    ],
    "conflict_note": "None apparent from the supplied profile."
  }
}
